A||N||40||stimpy||How much freedom can you give up?||||5||
Z||000000||stimpy||09-12-2001||06:43 PM||||Although I am, of course, shocked and appalled by the attack on our soil, I am not surprised. We as Americans demand the convenience and freedom to travel as we please. Our borders are so easy to cross, it was inevitable that these human vermin would creep into our homes.<BR>Now we are forever changed. There will be no "business as usual". And I think that we as a country need to realize that. One thing that really needs to change in an extreme manner is airport security. We have minimum wage earners armed with metal detecting wands standing between terrorists and the flying bombs which are our airliners.<BR>I propose that our airports need the same security as our Air Force bases. I would like to see our National Guard standing sentinal with loaded and cocked automatic weapons. I would be willing to do without carry on luggage. I would be willing to be subject to body pat downs and random searches. Is this too extreme? Can we as a nation accept these kind of intrusions into our privacy and freedom in the name of national security?  <P>Just pondering...||||reg||5
Z||000001||My7Fieros||09-12-2001||06:47 PM||||Well said, Stimpy, I agree 100%. Whatever it takes..............<P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B><I>My 7 Fieros</FONT></FONT></B></I>||||reg||14
Z||000002||Fformula88||09-12-2001||06:55 PM||||There is this funny thing about personal freedom... and that the right to it ends when it infringes on others rights.  Always has.  <P>In the light of these events every security measure deemed necessary should be put into place.  Of course, no amount of security can totally prevent problems.  Even with these measures, the hijackers may not have been stopped.  But it is worth tightening down!  <P>As for borders, maybe it is time to make them tougher to cross too.<P>------------------<BR>John  <BR>AOLIM: Fformula88<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero2m6siga.JPG">   <BR>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||09-12-2001||06:57 PM||||Although some measures can be taken, I think part of the problem is believing we can always stop them before the plane takes off. All flights should have a sky marshall on board from now on. Chances are very high that had an armed marshall been on each of those flights the terrorist would not have been successful.<P>Also, from the news reports it seems at least some of the hijackers had known terrorists links and were traveling under their own names. Why can't we create a black ball list and not let these people on board?||||reg||1
Z||000004||DaRkLoRD||09-12-2001||06:58 PM||||I'm generally against most restrictions on my personal freedom, but in light of this... I'd be more than happy to leave my pocket knife at home, and carry just a book and a bottle of water onto a plane instead of my whole backpack, the next time I fly somewhere.. and so what if they search my bag? oh no! t-shirts, jeans, socks.. underwear etc.. and OMG, toothpaste!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>and I'd definitely feel safer with armed military personell on board, or sky marshals, whatever they're called..<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000005||loafer87gt||09-12-2001||06:59 PM||||I think for starters we need to seriously revamp our aircraft security. I guy over at another forum had a good idea to prevent future hijacking. Instead of having one flight compartment for both the passengers and crew, have a solid wall between the pilots and the passenger with a seperate door for each chamber. This would prevent hijackers from disturbing the pilot and if they did harm and of the passengers, they would know that the pilot (who is safe in the front cabin) is going to be able to land the plane at the nearest airport where authorities will be able to deal with the culprits. I also think we are to the point where there should be no carry on luggage aboard aircraft and passengers should be searched before boarding the aircraft. This may sound extreme, but I think we are going to have to start taking some notes from our Israeli friends and becoming a little more paranoid. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||DaRkLoRD||09-12-2001||07:03 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DRH:<BR><B>Also, from the news reports it seems at least some of the hijackers had known terrorists links and were traveling under their own names. Why can't we create a black ball list and not let these people on board?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>and what happens if somewhere there's a 5 year old kid named Timmy McVeigh? etc   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> they'd need to be positively identified by their appearance as well as a name.. I've read several articles in popular science about new facial scanners that are able to determine known criminal faces with a pretty good degree of accuracy, easily well enough to alert security to make a human identification.<P>------------------<BR>     <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>     <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A><p>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 09-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tonker||09-12-2001||07:09 PM||||I agree Stimpy. It's even worse here. I'm not sure if any of you guys have heard the rumours, but I've heard that some of the bastards got into the States through Canada. I can certainly believe it. We have some of the most ridiculously lax security restrictions in the world. It's about time our government got with it. They're not only putting Canadians at risk, but also US citizens via our open border.<P>Hopefully this will serve to open the eyes of the socialists and bleeding hearts here about what happens when you open the gates to everyone.||||reg||13
Z||000008||Tonker||09-12-2001||07:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by loafer87gt:<BR><B>I think for starters we need to seriously revamp our aircraft security. I guy over at another forum had a good idea to prevent future hijacking. Instead of having one flight compartment for both the passengers and crew, have a solid wall between the pilots and the passenger with a seperate door for each chamber. This would prevent hijackers from disturbing the pilot and if they did harm and of the passengers, they would know that the pilot (who is safe in the front cabin) is going to be able to land the plane at the nearest airport where authorities will be able to deal with the culprits. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sunds like a good idea, something like the watertight bulkheads on ships. I had wondered why they don't do something like that for the flight deck at the very least. A bulletproof wall and door would have prevented this.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Steve Normington||09-12-2001||07:19 PM||||<FONT SIZE="3"><B>NO!</B></FONT><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR>They that can give up essential liberty to obtain a little temporary safety deserve neither liberty nor safety. <HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR><I>Benjamin Franklin</I><P>Trading freedoms for apparant safety will always cost us more in the long run. This tragedy could not have been stopped unless we were willing to submit to extreme measures.<P>No carry-on baggage? Hide a plastic knife in your shoe. Random ID checks? The hijackers had valid ID or else they wouldn't have been able to get boarding passes. Strip search 1 in 5 passengers? Two planes would still have been hijacked. Armed guards in the terminal? What signs would these guards use to stop the hijackers?<P>We as a people keep trading liberty for security. We get less of the former, but never get any more of the latter.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||DaRkLoRD||09-12-2001||07:19 PM||||I work at a canadian nuclear facility... and let me just say I agree with you guys about Canada's pathetic security.... I won't go into details for obvious reasons.<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000011||DaRkLoRD||09-12-2001||07:22 PM||||I don't think having the cockpit inaccessible to the cabin, and having armed guards on board flights, is limiting any of our freedom..... yet that's almost all it would take.<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Raydar||09-12-2001||07:26 PM||||All interesting posts.<P>I wouldn't mind not having a carry-on. I also think that having armed FAA cops on all flights is a good idea. <BR>One thing about the situation at hand, though, is that the knives that they used to effect these hijackings were probably legal. <BR>I was amazed that knives were legal to carry on. I didn't even think about it until I was in line at the security gate in Portland, and I reached into my pocket and found my pocket knife that I had forgotten to pack.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> I just threw it in the bowl with my keys. Nobody said a word.<BR>I do think we need to restrict our borders. Pretty much anybody can just come in to the US. I haven't completely made up my mind on this one, though.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Hank is Here||09-12-2001||07:34 PM||||I will not give up any freedoms guaranteed  to be by the Constitution.   I DO NOT WANT TO LIVE IN A POLICE STATE and will not let any terrorists make this a country one!||||reg||13
Z||000014||Formula||09-12-2001||07:42 PM||||they had to be at least intimidating to hijack a plane with a BOX CUTTER....<P>I have 10 million BOX CUTTERS at home because I used to work in a grocery store.  I slit my hand open millions of times with these things and I personally would not be afraid of the damage one could do to me... most likely wouldnt be lethal unless it was a major artery.  But they also had 3-6 hijackers on each flight so yu would have to get everyone involved to stop them.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Cheever3000||09-12-2001||07:56 PM||||I agree, stimpy, but I think the passengers of all 4 of the hijacked planes should have overcome the hijackers, like those on the one plane tried to do.<P>I also think more people should stop to give assistance to motorists on the side of the road who are obviously in need of help.<P>I'm dreaming.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||maryjane||09-12-2001||07:57 PM||||Yep Tonker, the fact that some of the terrorists entered thru Canada is in all the news. Being called  "the Canadian connection".<BR>At least two of the slugs were on the Imigration & Naturalization (INS)'watchlist'.<BR>Don't know how they got thru.<BR> I don't think there should be any carry-ons allowed, but hope they improve the baggage service. There is no need for a pocket knife or any other weapon on board an A/C||||reg||1
Z||000017||Steve Normington||09-12-2001||08:01 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DaRkLoRD:<BR><B>I don't think having the cockpit inaccessible to the cabin, and having armed guards on board flights, is limiting any of our freedom..... yet that's almost all it would take.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I agree, those would not be limiting freedoms. I am against the random searches, pat downs, etc.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||Wannabe||09-12-2001||08:13 PM||||Frequently, when disasters happen, retrospect shows just how easily they could have been prevented. The Titanic comes to mind. In this case, the attackers were not heavily armed, and would have been foiled by an armed guard, or even an effort by the passengers, had they know they were going to die anyway. At least one of these guys showed up with a UAE passport, and bought a first class ticket with cash shorlty before departure. How could they have missed checking hi out very carefully? With new and reasonable safety precautions, and diligent exercise of those already in place, and the watchful eyes of a stunned society, it should be possible to to prevent this kind of thing from happening in the future. Next step will be to watch out for other types of attacks that can happen in unsecured areas, like subways or major sporting events. ||||reg||1
Z||000019||Fierochic88||09-12-2001||08:36 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Wannabe:<BR><B> The Titanic comes to mind.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Eerily those people jumping out of the building reminded me of the Titanic....for anyone that has ever seen the movie they would understand....Way to creepy for me.  I can't believe the news actually showed that stuff!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000020||stimpy||09-12-2001||10:22 PM||||Steve, I feel where you are coming from. Believe me, I'm usually pretty liberal in my views. But as our own airliners have become weapons of mass destruction, they needed to be treated as such and guarded appropriately. I mean, you've given up your liberty to go strolling into nuclear powerplants for the safety of our country from sabotage. No doubt ol Ben Franklin was a wise man, I don't think he was thinking about recent developments.<BR>I do appreciate your input, definately food for thought.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000021||Jaygee79||09-12-2001||10:38 PM||||I would be happy to not have a carry-on.  I like the idea of having an armed officer on every plane, but the thing that bothers me about that is this:  If I'm a hijacker and I'm able to catch the officer off guard or something and knock him down, you've just basically supplied me with my weapon. i don't have to worry about trying to sneak something through security, i can just get it on the plane.  i'm sure most of the time this wouldn't happen, but it certainly could.  i also like the idea of having the cockpit sealed off.  i would like to hear from a pilot if that would have any negative effects on safety or what not.<P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="purple">Ranger Jaime<BR>AOL IM: Jaygee79<BR>ICQ: 85931334</FONT><BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/jaygee79/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Jaygee79's Web Page</A><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/peggypic.jpg"> <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif"> <p>[This message has been edited by Jaygee79 (edited 09-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000022||Cheever3000||09-12-2001||11:03 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>i would like to hear from a pilot if that would have any negative effects on safety or what not.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE>I'm not a pilot, but I have had the opportunity on 3 occassions to ride in the cockpit of Airborne Express planes.  They are DC-8's, DC-9's, and 767's, with the passenger seats removed to allow cargo containers.  While the cockpits are cramped, there is little need for the crew to exit the cabin.  From what I've seen, there is no reason why there could not be a great deal of improvement to increased security for airline crews.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000023||Jaygee79||09-12-2001||11:13 PM||||if that is the case then i am all for it.||||reg||1
Z||000024||DaRkLoRD||09-12-2001||11:16 PM||||So have a couple of uniformed officers.. and a couple more plainclothes ones..<P>anyone else read Rainbow Six? (first chapter/intro)<P>------------------<BR>     <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>     <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A><p>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 09-12-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000025||Mach10||09-13-2001||01:37 AM||||The guards don't even have to be military. Municipal cops, even just Airport security. Have one or two in each section with a flak jacket and weapon. It's as much a deterrant as anything. If they are able to overpower a trained security guard/officer, who has been trained specifically in hand-hand combat and grappling (I sugest Hapkido), then nothing would have stopped them before. So the Airline has to swallow another few seat-costs. Less hijacks, though. That's gotta be worth something in the long-run.||||reg||14
Z||000026||artherd||09-13-2001||03:49 AM||||"Any man willing to give up a little freedom for some temporary safety, deserves neither."<P>-Benjamin Franklin, I belive.  NO, I am not willing to give up personal freedom to have some token notion that I am 'safer'.<P>I am on the other hand all for sky-marshal type guards.  I would like the piolits and crew to be carrying P90 FN assault guns and be trained in their use. (though I am not happy about it, it is an area where such security is needed, just like a powerplant or an armoured car, so put the guards in place darnit!)<P>But *none* of it will stop or even detur anyone willing to fly their own plane into the ground at 600mph.   Death does not intimidate people planning to die anyway.  It won't work.<P>I would not want to be the leader of any country right now, much less the US.<BR>The decisions we now face are horroffic.<P>I belive the only disuasion to terrorism would requre that we reduce *every* nation that is currently harbouring terrorists to fused glass.  AKA nuke the hell out of them, innocents included (though in Afganastan and Libia I figure we stand a 50-50 chance of hitting a terrorist...)<P>It's simple, terrorise the free world, and your *country* ceases to exist. entirely.<P>Of course, this would make us all no better than the terrorists we claim to abhor...<P>You can see why I am glad I don't have the job of deciding for real.  I do not envy those that do, and I wish them the best.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000027||Cliff Pennock||09-13-2001||04:44 AM||||Tightening security does not make a police-state, nor are you giving up freedom. A good example is Monaco. Monaco has cameras on every street corner, and there are a lot of uniformed police officers on the streets. The result? Crime is non-existent, the people feel very safe and their freedom is actually enhanced. They have the freedom to walk around in the middle of the night with loads of money on them, they have the freedom to leave their cars unlocked with their personal belongins in them, and are never limited by fear of something happening.<P>I hear that argument a lot, here in Holland as well. Crime is getting out of hand here. Some suggested we need to place surveillance camera in areas where crime is at its highest. It finds a lot of opposition because people are shouting it is limiting to their freedom. That's BS. I would love to have the freedom to walk around at night where I live. I can't do that now because chances are high I'll get mugged. I would love to have the freedom to accidently forget something of value in my car, without the almost 100% chance there is now that it will be stolen the next day. My car was once stolen from a parking lot. There were cameras there but law prohibited that it was taped. So they were never able to find out who actually stole the car. They caught a guy who was driving it but since they couldn't proof he was the one that stole it, he was set free within a day, and I ended up paying the damage to the car ($1500) myself.<P>I wish people would understand that better surveillance does not limit someone in it's freedom. Unless of course, you think that people need the freedom to commit crime.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000028||Hank is Here||09-13-2001||09:41 AM||||Cliff, <BR>I have to disagree.  At for what I believe in surveillance is not good, this just lets the government come more and more into your personal life.  Once you put cameras on every street corner it creates a "Big Brother" type of feeling. Having more security officers and airline marshals are not the beginning of a police state?  Well I ask you then where does a police state start? We slowly loose our freedom and nobody notices unit we are under control.  I will not let terrorists indirectly take my freedoms.  I carry my old Swiss army knife with me everywhere, now not one airlines; what B.S. is that!  Not everyone may like what I am saying but speak now or your freedom will be gone forever.  <P>Hank    ||||reg||1
Z||000029||Voytek||09-13-2001||11:50 AM||||I believe there is a fine line between 'security' and 'infringement on freedom'.  At which point do cameras become the government's tool to spy on people?<P>Safety is definitely a concern but raising a crime-free society starts at home and at school.<P>In the 50's and still in the 60's countries had NO cameras on the streets and people were able to leave their homes unlocked, car windows open and stroll around some of the darkest streets without the fear of being mugged (no, I'm not that old but this is what I'm told by those who remember).<P>Cameras are a solution to an existing problem but do not eliminate the cause of it.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000030||deceler8||09-13-2001||12:06 PM||||I think we've failed to acknowledge the loss of freedom that the terrorists have suffered as well.<P>There are a lot of folks now who cannot travel or even move within the nations that have prior to this week supported them. <P>The fear of further attack has resulted in the loss of some of the freedoms we took for granted. Same for the terrorists. Many are now marked men, as the countries and individuals who supported them to date now fear imminent and horrible reprisal both from their countrymen and the forces of the United States.||||reg||1
Z||000031||LZeitgeist||09-13-2001||12:14 PM||||In public areas, the only people who have anything to fear from cameras are those who intend to do wrong. <P>Having cameras in private areas (inside homes, in restroom stalls, etc.) would be an affront on our privacy. However, having cameras to cover and protect areas of public interaction would only be a negative affect to those who would be trying to 'get away with something'. <P>If people have no intention of wrongdoing, then they should have no concern about 'being watched'. ||||reg||1
Z||000032||ray b||09-13-2001||01:31 PM||||guns on planes can be very dangerius to every one on the plane if used, maybe a 22/hollowpoints but even those can hole the skin of the plane with bad results.<BR>sealing cockpits may work but then the crews are traped without bathrooms or food/drink or relive on long trips new planes maybe but refits are very hard to do.<BR>ben f was right and now look out for a ton of new laws and rules for your own good!!!!!<BR>like cars searched at airports how does that prevent a hi-jack with more to come soon.<BR>BIG BROTHER IS GETTING BIGGER NOW<BR>yes we need to do something but how far is toooo far or not enuff. like a true nut would hide a knife up his rear end but do we want to do strip search with body cavaty inspections to get on a plane????<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000033||DRH||09-13-2001||02:07 PM||||I think too much focus is being put on eliminating weapons. This group seems to have invested enough time and effort to learn how to fly a jumbo jet at least well enough to accurately fly it into a building. What makes anyone think the next group won't invest enough time into hand to hand combat training to be able to effectively take over a plane with their bare hands?||||reg||1
Z||000034||deceler8||09-13-2001||02:14 PM||||One of the measures that might prove successful would be routinely canceling flights that have very few passengers.<P>None of the flights involved had more than 70 passengers aboard...most were less than a third full. What I still haven't heard is whether these flights were fully booked or not. I suspect they were, otherwise they might have been routinely canceled for economic reasons.<P>A fully-booked flight with few passengers showing up at the gate has to be considered a danger sign in the future.||||reg||1
Z||000035||AkursedX||09-13-2001||02:23 PM||||Well, look at this from an economic standpoint......Ok, we hire guards for every flight, we hire extra security at airports, we have trained, well paid people running the check points.  <P>All these people have to trained and paid.  The cost of that would be immense!  And it will all translate into what I would predict to be a significant increase in the price of a plane ticket.  <P>Now of course in this time right now, I am sure everyone will be willing to pay some extra money to pay for flights.  But after this settles down, I feel this will result in significant decrease in the amount of people flying, which will probably result in a number of lost jobs.  <P>There is always a price for freedom, but there is even more of a price for security.  Are we willing to pay it?  That will be the question.  <P>As for me, I don't fly anyways.  <P><P>------------------<BR><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000036||frontal lobe||09-13-2001||02:28 PM||||It's not just giving up the revenue in seats as the cost of having security on airplanes.  I assume they would want to be paid???  Yet people want cheap air fare.<P>And where are they going to get that many high quality security people?  Ever see the "security" personnel around?  Big ol' guts.  Barney Fife types, too.  It's not like they are going to be able to hire Arnold, Steven Segall (spelling?), and Bruce Willis.  Reportedly some of the hijackers had mace.  Would it be that hard to overwhelm a security person if they were willing to die like they were?  We can spend TONS of money on this, but would it have prevented what happened.<P>Regarding the passengers not taking over the planes again:  I don't know what they should have done.  Definitely when told they were going to die, they should have tried.  I don't know on some flights if they didn't know, and they were thinking it was just a hijacking and they would land somewhere and negotiate and all that.  That seemed to be more common with hijackings than using them as intentional bombs.<P>Regarding surveillance, I would of course prefer not to have it, but on the other hand if you live your life in an honest way, what you have to fear from surveillance is MUCH smaller than the gain you get.||||reg||1
Z||000037||84Bill||09-13-2001||02:28 PM||||<B>NONE!</B><BR>I am not willing to give up my freedom for a "safe" place to live. "Safe and Secure" are words used to describe the impossible. I was never safe, rather my illusion of safety has faltered a bit over the last few days. I can tell you that when I drive to work every day I am not safe, my life is in danger the moment I enter our roadways. I am not nor have I ever been truly safe but I have been blinded and jaded. I am in no way trying to diminish the horrible events we watched unfold. <BR>To restrict the freedome of all to ensure this sort of thing never happens again in my eyes is unacceptable since there is NO WAY TO STOP IT save one! There are to many ways around the system, to many ways to enter an aircraft, to many ways to KILL someone to create TERROR and once terror is in place the terrorist is in complete control. <BR>The brave souls on the aircraft that crashed in PA are heros in every sense of the word. The stood and stared right into the face of terror and fought back and sacreficed their lives in the process. They knew they were going to die and decided to do it on their OWN terms. I hail them.  <BR>What needs to be done? I don't know but I would most likely rather FIGHT for my freedom than just give it up for an illusion. Give the same situation as those aboard the doomed aircraft I may have rode it out like all but the ones that crashed in PA. NOW I will fight to the death just as they did!! I know I may die but I will not just sit back and assume I am safe as I have for 35+ years. It is up to all of us to fight back in a hijacking situation, we dont need more security measures at our airports we just need more americans to say "here, hold this beer for me" It takes a madman to defeat a madman but an evil madman will not win. <BR>6 candyassed men armed only with terror are no match for 84 good decent pissed off American yahoos stuffed in an aluminum tube with nowhere to hide. They would be pulp in seconds!!<P>SAFETY IS AN ILLUSION! <BR>MANY GOOD MEN DIED ALREADY TO ENSURE MY FREEDOM, WE SHOULD HONOR THEM AND NOT GIVE UP THE FIGHT FOR FREEDOM ON OUR WATCH.<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/flag.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000038||Jaygee79||09-14-2001||01:31 AM||||think about this.  are you really giving up a freedom?  there is no fundamental right to luggage in the Constitution.  if it doesn't infringe upon my rights as listed in the Constitution, then if I have to give it up for the safety of myself and/or others then that's what i have to do.<P>and anyway, most things we take for granted as rights or freedoms are really not that.  they are privelidges.  the government owns the airports.  they can pass a law that no luggage whatsoever is allowed on a plane.  or an airline can ban carry-ons from their planes.<p>[This message has been edited by Jaygee79 (edited 09-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000039||Mach10||09-14-2001||02:11 AM||||I'd give up a lot to be completely safe where I live. What good is total freedom if my picket fence has razor wire on it, and I have to keep a gun by the door? I'd rather not have to keep a gun at all. Cameras in public offer a problem only if you are doing ill **** . Security SHOULD be tightened at airports, and on airplanes. Use formed power slugs. They disintegrate if they hit anything hard. They don't ricochet, and they don't punch holes through metal. <P>This of course, is my opinion only. ||||reg||12
Z||000040||stsmithgt||09-14-2001||07:56 PM||||The expense of having a guard on every plane  along with more gaurds in the airport would be pretty high.  It's not like they would be posing as a flight attendants or custodians so they can multitask.<P>About these loss of freedoms....<BR>I don't want to loose anymore freedoms.  I'd rather have face recognition cameras in the airport since the government knows most of the problem child terrorists.  My last flight was about 2 years ago and they didn't even blink at this knife I brought on. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/knife.jpg">  I thought they were going to tell me to leave it behind, but they thought nothing of it.  I don't think I'll get away with that this time out.  <BR>I've already had to reconfigure my SKS rifle that I bought several accessories, all legal at the time and not cheap.  It's back to it's crappy wooden stock with nobody to cover the parts I had to discard.  Now it's only ergonomicaly correct for someone around 12 years old because of people scared of their own shadow and think restriction on law biding citizens will make it a safer world.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> <BR>Don't be victoms of your own fear.  There has to be a better solution to this problem without draining out pocket books in the process.||||reg||1
